  "query_preview": "(This evening through Wednesday)...\nIssued at 217 PM EDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nLatest surface analysis depicts surface high pressure centered\nnear the East Coast while surface ridging extends back into the \nMidwest. Meanwhile, satellite imagery depicts extensive mid-high \nclouds from a subtle shortwave moving through aloft and moisture \nfrom the remnants of Hurricane Polo streaming in. \n\nACARS soundings show a dry layer remains in the lower levels \nacross central Indiana from residual subsidence induced dry air. \nThis will likely limit any chance for measurable precipitation, \nbut a few sprinkles are possible over northern portions of the \narea where there is greater mid-upper level moisture. Less cloud \ncover across southern counties has allowed for temperatures to \nwarm well into the mid 80s while greater cloud cover further north\nhas kept temperatures generally in the upper 70s to low 80s. \n\nSurface ridging along with lingering subsidence induced dry air in \nthe lower levels will likely allow dry conditions to persist for \nmuch of the day Thursday, outside of the potential for a few brief \nsprinkles. Highs are expected to be well above normal on Thursday \ndue to increasing warm air advection, ranging from the mid to upper \n80s.\n\nThursday night through Friday...\n\nModel guidance generally depicts the remnants of Hurricane Polo \ngetting absorbed into a broad upper level trough moving across the \nGreat Lakes Region later this week. Deep tropical surging northward \nand sufficient forcing from the merging features supports increasing \nrain chances. Precipitation is expected to begin as early as Thursday \nevening across western portions of the area with widespread rain \npossible Thursday night into Friday morning. \n\nModels have continued to trend towards lower QPF due to overall \nforcing quickly diminishing as the remnants of Hurricane Polo get \nabsorbed into the upper wave. At this time, it appears most \nlocations should see around 0.25 to 0.75 inches of rain. Localized \namounts up to an inch or slightly higher are still possible. Look \nfor an associated cold front to push through Friday morning. Cooler-\ndrier air filtering into the region through the day will support \nmostly dry conditions by the afternoon and clearing skies. Much \ncooler highs in the upper 60s to low 70s are expected on Friday \nthanks to the cooler airmass filtering in. \n\nFriday night through the middle of next week...\n\nHigh pressure building in Friday night into Saturday will provide \nquiet weather conditions with partly cloudy skies. Guidance is in \ngood agreement that another cold front associated with an upper wave \npushes through around late Sunday. Precipitation is not expected due \nto limited moisture return ahead of the front, but look for another \nshot of cooler-drier air. \n\nHighs generally in the mid 60s to low 70s over the weekend are \nexpected to fall into the 60s for all of central Indiana by early \nnext week. Efficient radiational cooling conditions from surface \nhigh pressure overhead Monday night could allow for temperatures to \nfall into the upper 30s across northeastern counties. Patchy frost \ncannot be ruled out. Surface high pressure shifts east towards the \nmiddle of next week allowing for return flow. Large scale subsidence \ncontinues to support dry weather while return flow supports warming \ntemperatures.",
